<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Triumvirate</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Triumvirate was the political alliance formed in 43 BCE by Octavian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Lepidus to rule the Roman state after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. It defeated Caesar's assassins and carried out proscriptions of its opponents. Its breakdown led to the war between Octavian and Antony, from which Octavian emerged as Augustus, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate.jpg" alt="Second Triumvirate" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: You may select the license of your choice. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antony_with_Octavian_aureus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Second Triumvirate was an extraordinary commission and magistracy created at the end of the Roman republic for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, Lepidus and Octavian. The name is a modern misnomer, derived from the branding of the alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> and Caesar as the First Triumvirate, and it first appears in the late 17th century. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> was assassinated on 15 March 44 BCE, the liberatores and the remaining supporters of Caesar reached a settlement, but Antony pushed through legislation assigning himself the provinces of Cisalpine and Transalpine Gaul.</p><p>Octavian reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 19 August 43 BCE and secured election as consul. At the urging of the soldiers he reconciled with Antony under the protection of Lepidus, and the three established themselves for five years. The lex Titia confirmed this on 27 November 43 BCE, giving them power to issue binding edicts, to act without appeal and to appoint magistrates and provincial governors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The triumvirs issued a death list of proscribed men. Ancient sources differ on the number: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> gives figures of 200, a bit more than 200 and 300, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> 130 senators, and Florus 140. François Hinard believes about 300 men were proscribed, split roughly evenly between senators and equestrians. In 42 BCE Antony and Octavian crossed to Macedonia with some forty legions, and at Philippi the forces of Brutus and Cassius were defeated. Antony then reorganised the eastern provinces, while Octavian settled veterans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sextus-pompey">Sextus Pompey</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p>Unrest over land for veterans led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perusine-war">Perusine War</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-antonius">Lucius Antonius</a> was besieged at Perusia; Octavian sacked the town and burnt it. The Treaty of Brundisium in September 40 BCE confirmed Antony as master of the east and Octavian in the west, with Lepidus keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The triumvirate was renewed in 37 BCE, and in 36 BCE Octavian forced Lepidus out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The triumvirate was formally constituted for five years, renewed in 37 BCE and expired in 32 BCE. Its powers exceeded those of ordinary consuls, and the Capitoline Fasti listed the triumvirs above the consuls. After Lepidus fell, Octavian prepared for a showdown with Antony and worked to link him with Cleopatra, whose relationship with Antony was unpopular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Relations between the two broke down, and they fought a final war, from which Octavian emerged the victor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Triumvirate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Julius Caesar's crossing of the Rubicon in 49 BCE, his assassination in 44 BCE and the wars of the <b>Second Triumvirate</b> ended in Octavian's decisive victory over Mark Antony and Cleopatra VII at the Battle of Actium in 31 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Triumvirate</b> of Mark Antony, Octavian and Lepidus defeated the assassins at Philippi in 42 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>San Martín's Lautaro Lodge and the Patriotic Society overthrew it on 8 October 1812 for the <b>Second Triumvirate</b>, which convened the General Constituent and Sovereign Assembly of 1813.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Proscribed by the <b>Second Triumvirate</b>, Marcus Tullius Cicero was overtaken by soldiers near Formiae and killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-7">7 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Gervasio Antonio de Posadas, a lawyer who had sat in the <b>Second Triumvirate</b>, became the first Supreme Director of the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata, the executive office set up that year by the Asamblea del Año XIII.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-31">31 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Off Naulochus in Sicily, Octavian's admiral Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa defeated the fleet of Sextus Pompey, son of Pompey the Great and last leader of organised opposition to the <b>Second Triumvirate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-3">3 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
